---
import Image from "astro/components/Image.astro";
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import timeConverter from "../../assets/projects/gnss-time.png";
import visibilityPlanner from "../../assets/projects/gnss-vis.png";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="GNSS Calculator"
  description="A browser toolkit for satellite-navigation work: convert between GNSS time scales and coordinate frames, plan observations, and inspect RINEX, NMEA and ANTEX files."
  subtitle="A browser toolkit for satellite-navigation work: convert between time scales and coordinate frames, plan observations, and inspect the files a receiver actually produces."
  badges={["Web app", "Open source engine", "Free, no account"]}
  actions={[
    { label: "Open gnsscalc.com", href: "https://gnsscalc.com", primary: true },
    { label: "gnss-js on GitHub", href: "https://github.com/MiguelPuntoEs/gnss-js" },
  ]}
>
  <figure slot="hero" class="case">
    <Image
      src={timeConverter}
      alt="The GNSS Calculator time converter, showing GPS, Galileo, BeiDou, UNIX, GLONASS, Julian and TAI representations of the same instant"
      widths={[600, 1200]}
      sizes="(min-width: 640px) 600px, 100vw"
      loading="eager"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The time converter. One instant, expressed at once in GPS, Galileo, BeiDou, UNIX,
      GLONASS, Julian and TAI — with the current leap-second offsets shown inline
      (GPS − UTC = 18 s, TAI − UTC = 37 s).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="section case">
    <h2 class="section__title">Why it exists</h2>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <p>
        Satellite navigation has no single clock. GPS counts seconds from 6 January 1980 and
        ignores leap seconds; Galileo and BeiDou each start from their own epoch; receivers log
        UNIX timestamps; astronomers and orbit models want Julian dates; and the offsets between
        all of them drift as leap seconds are added. Getting one of these conversions wrong by
        18 seconds is an easy mistake to make and an expensive one to debug.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual answer is a spreadsheet someone made years ago, or a handful of separate
        single-purpose pages. GNSS Calculator puts the conversions, the observation-file viewers
        and the planning tools in one place, and runs them in the browser so nothing has to be
        uploaded anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section case">
    <h2 class="section__title">What's in it</h2>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <p>Twelve tools, in four groups:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Time</b> — conversion across GPS, Galileo, BeiDou, GLONASS, UNIX, Julian Date, MJD,
          UTC, TAI and TT; GPS week and time-of-week; differences between two epochs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Position</b> — a positioning calculator, coordinate transforms between ECEF and
          geodetic frames, and a satellite visibility planner.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Analyze</b> — quality control and solution inspection for observation files, plus
          RINEX, NMEA, ANTEX and spectrum viewers, and a format converter.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Monitor</b> — live NTRIP stream connection and current broadcast-ephemeris health.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <Image
        src={visibilityPlanner}
        alt="The satellite visibility planner, with inputs for latitude, longitude, height, date, duration and elevation mask"
        widths={[600, 1200]}
        sizes="(min-width: 640px) 600px, 100vw"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The visibility planner predicts which satellites clear your elevation mask over a chosen
        window, along with the visible-satellite count and PDOP, by downloading IGS broadcast
        ephemeris for that day and propagating each orbit.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section case">
    <h2 class="section__title">How it works</h2>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <p>
        Everything runs client-side. Observation files you open — RINEX, NMEA, ANTEX — are parsed
        in the browser and never leave the machine, which matters when the data is a survey you
        don't own. Orbit work uses IGS broadcast ephemeris for the requested day, falling back to
        public almanacs for dates beyond its coverage.
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculation engine is published separately as <a
          class="link link--ext"
          href="https://github.com/MiguelPuntoEs/gnss-js"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">gnss-js<svg class="icon"><use href="#i-arrow-out" /></svg></a
        >, so the conversions can be used without the interface.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section case">
    <h2 class="section__title">Details</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div class="fact">
        <dt>Type</dt><dd>Web application, runs entirely in the browser</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="fact">
        <dt>Audience</dt><dd>Surveyors, geodesists, GNSS engineers and researchers</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="fact">
        <dt>Data sources</dt><dd>IGS broadcast ephemeris, public almanacs, NTRIP casters</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="fact">
        <dt>Source</dt>
        <dd>
          <a class="link" href="https://github.com/MiguelPuntoEs/gnss-js" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            MiguelPuntoEs/gnss-js
          </a>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="fact"><dt>Cost</dt><dd>Free, no account required</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
